      "notes": "Conduit now evaluates what it ships, and an architecture review caught the repo overclaiming: the guardrail engine is not wired to any request path, so the docs that called it an enforced floor were corrected in place and the injection grade dropped to weak. The judge is measured rather than assumed. On a 30-case class-balanced set, groundedness judging reaches Cohen's kappa 0.93 and is the one pair the repo claims; relevance judging sits at 0.13 and is explicitly barred from gating output until tuned. The cheap judge model turned out to reject every answer, which a raw catch rate would have shown as flawless. Guardrails catch every attack in the golden set (recall 1.00) while refusing 4 of 16 safe inputs (precision 0.83), and a default-deny agent loop means a side-effecting tool cannot run without explicit authority. Pillar math: Product Judgment = avg(D1 good, D2 good, D3 good, D6 good, D7 good, S4 good, S6 good, R1 good, API1 good) = 7.0; System Design = avg(S1 good, S2 great, S3 great, S5 good, S8 good, AG1 good, AG2 good, AG4 great, API1 good, API3 weak) = 7.6; Evaluation = avg(E1 good, E2 good, E3 good, E4 good, E5 good) = 7.0; Reliability & Ownership = avg(D4 good, B1 good, B2 great, B3 good, B5 good, SH3 good, SH8 weak, SH10 good) = 7.0; Safety = avg(E3 good, SH1 weak, SH2 great, SH7 good, SH9 weak, SH10 good, AG4 great) = 7.0; Economics = avg(S1 good, AG2 good) = 7.0; Communication = avg(D1 good, SH4 weak, API2 good, API3 weak, D5 great, SH5 good) = 6.5; UX/UI & Interaction = avg(S4 good) = 7.0; Collaboration & Stakeholders = avg(D5 great, SH5 good, CS1 great) = 9.0. Overall = round(mean of 9 non-null pillars (7.233) x 10) = 72. Collaboration & Stakeholders is null: no stakeholder question was graded this run, so it stays a visible gap rather than a guess.",
